930da6-f008-59fd-875c-d797cf29d182" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278293 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278293 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278293 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278293 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ 4u+6v\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278293 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "18d1f220-8bd2-586b-90ca-87751084600f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278293 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ and $y=4u+6v.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\,\\text{ }4\\, & \\,\\text{ }6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\cdot 6-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v))\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278296 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278296 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160427" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278296 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278296 #edb/ref 1071 "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278296 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "861c1236-2662-5b7d-b150-03b6774d0f17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278296 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-2v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-4uv-4uv \\\\  & =-8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,1)}=-8(-2)(1)=16.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278297 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278297 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278297 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278297 #edb/ref 1071 "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278297 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19415143-780a-5182-8164-c1af6838225c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278297 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}u & -v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =u\\cdot u-(-v)\\cdot v \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,-2)}=(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}=5.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278298 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278298 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160431" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278298 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278298 #edb/ref 1071 "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2}+uv \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278298 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "876a5d15-9cbd-5150-8246-c71dbabaa45c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278298 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={v}^{2}+uv$ and $y(u,v)=v-u.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v+u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & 2v+u \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-(2v+u)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =v+2v+u \\\\  & =3v+u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(8,-2)}=3(-2)+8=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278597 #edb/ref 1080 "12\\,\\delta A" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278597 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278597 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "018385f7-7519-5922-8545-718d1e4443e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278688 #edb/ref 1080 "6\\,\\delta A" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278688 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592